            description: "Fruit Notes gives mixed grower observations for Regina. Source notes: Weaver listed it as a favorite but said yield consistency was a big problem; Cherry Hill listed it among favorites; Terhune said it looked good but did not taste good there and was poorly timed for a small one-day PYO harvest; Milburn called it the best sweet cherry grown on the East Coast because of dark red crunchy fruit, long stems, late bloom, and little cracking, but noted a vigorous hard-to-train tree, need for late-blooming pollinizers such as Gold and Black Gold, and lower productivity than other varieties"
